    "math_academy_id": "q-181683",
    "knowledge_point_id": "5a3df69f-653e-5148-bd46-580097e43362",
    "knowledge_point": "Linear Boundaries, Two Conditions",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108214/assets/e2591e9da960008980cf96184975ef19713735814183a97ca8c8f784536fced5.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-x-3\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x+4\\}\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-3$ and *below* the line $y=x+4.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-3,\\,y<x+4.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x-3<y<x+4.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=-x-3$ and $y=x+4$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x-3\\le y\\le x+4\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x-3\\le y\\le x+4\\}.\n$$",
